<edge source="MATH6_C0002" target="MATH6_C0003">
  <data key="d4">0.7</data>
  <data key="d5">Number patterns are a specific kind of pattern; pedagogically students learn the general idea of patterns before identifying number patterns, but the supplied passages do not directly link the two, so review is advised.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0002" target="MATH6_C0004">
  <data key="d4">0.9</data>
  <data key="d5">The shape‑pattern passage explicitly calls them "patterns of shapes", showing that understanding the general notion of pattern precedes learning shape patterns.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7">CH01_EN_PDF009_B006</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0002" target="MATH6_C0005">
  <data key="d4">0.9</data>
  <data key="d5">Number sequences are described as "basic ... patterns", indicating that the broader concept of pattern is a prerequisite for studying sequences.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7">CH01_EN_PDF002_B014</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0002" target="MATH6_C0006">
  <data key="d4">0.85</data>
  <data key="d5">Odd numbers are listed among number sequences, which are presented as patterns; thus learners need the general pattern idea first.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7">CH01_EN_PDF003_B003</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0002" target="MATH6_C0007">
  <data key="d4">0.85</data>
  <data key="d5">Even numbers appear in the same list of number‑sequence patterns, so the general pattern concept underlies the even‑number concept.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7">CH01_EN_PDF003_B003</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0002" target="MATH6_C0008">
  <data key="d4">0.85</data>
  <data key="d5">Triangular numbers are presented as a type of number‑sequence pattern, requiring prior understanding of patterns.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7">CH01_EN_PDF003_B003</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0002" target="MATH6_C0009">
  <data key="d4">0.82</data>
  <data key="d5">Students first learn to recognise and describe patterns; square numbers are introduced as a specific visual pattern (dots forming a square grid). Understanding the general idea of patterns is therefore needed before identifying square‑number patterns.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7">CH01_EN_PDF001_B001;CH01_EN_PDF005_B005</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0002" target="MATH6_C0010">
  <data key="d4">0.81</data>
  <data key="d5">The chapter first discusses patterns and then lists cube numbers as another member of the family of number‑pattern sequences. Recognising a pattern is a prerequisite for grasping the cube‑number sequence.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7">CH01_EN_PDF001_B001;CH01_EN_PDF016_B016</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0002" target="MATH6_C0011">
  <data key="d4">0.78</data>
  <data key="d5">Powers of 2 are presented as a particular numeric pattern. Learners need the earlier notion of patterns to understand why the sequence 1, 2, 4, 8,… is called a pattern of powers of 2.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7">CH01_EN_PDF001_B001;CH01_EN_PDF003_B003</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0002" target="MATH6_C0040">
  <data key="d4">0.95</data>
  <data key="d5">Palindromes are introduced explicitly as a pattern in numbers, indicating that recognizing patterns is a prerequisite for understanding palindromic numbers.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7">CH03_EN_PDF007_B004;CH03_HI_PDF007_B004</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0002" target="MATH6_C0049">
  <data key="d4">0.92</data>
  <data key="d5">The later chapter explicitly asks learners to identify patterns in tables and numbers, building directly on the introductory concepts of patterns presented in Chapter 1; thus understanding patterns is pedagogically necessary before tackling these activities.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7">CH01_EN_PDF001_B003;CH03_EN_PDF003_B011</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0002" target="MATH6_ADD001">
  <data key="d4">0.92</data>
  <data key="d5">The pattern chapter introduces the idea of mathematical patterns, which is needed before studying specific numerical patterns such as Virahanka (Fibonacci) numbers presented later.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7">CH01_EN_PDF001_B001;CH01_EN_PDF013_B009</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0002" target="MATH6_ADD002">
  <data key="d4">0.78</data>
  <data key="d5">Collatz conjecture is presented as a mysterious numerical pattern; recognizing and working with such patterns presupposes the earlier general notion of patterns, though the textbook does not state it explicitly.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7">CH01_EN_PDF001_B001;CH03_EN_PDF014_B006</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0003" target="MATH6_C0005">
  <data key="d4">0.9</data>
  <data key="d5">Number patterns are essentially sequences of numbers; the sequence chapter builds on the earlier notion of number patterns, making the latter a prerequisite.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7">CH01_EN_PDF002_B014</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0003" target="MATH6_C0006">
  <data key="d4">0.86</data>
  <data key="d5">Number patterns introduce the idea of sequences; odd numbers are presented as a specific example, so students need the general pattern concept first.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7">CH01_EN_PDF003_B003</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0003" target="MATH6_C0007">
  <data key="d4">0.86</data>
  <data key="d5">The chapter first explains number patterns and then lists even numbers as a particular pattern, making the general concept a prerequisite.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7">CH01_EN_PDF003_B003</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0003" target="MATH6_C0008">
  <data key="d4">0.84</data>
  <data key="d5">Triangular numbers are introduced as one of many number patterns; understanding the broader notion of patterns is needed before studying this specific sequence.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7">CH01_EN_PDF005_B004</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0003" target="MATH6_C0009">
  <data key="d4">0.85</data>
  <data key="d5">Square numbers are treated as a type of number pattern; learners must first grasp what a pattern is to recognize square-number patterns.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7">CH01_EN_PDF005_B005</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0003" target="MATH6_C0010">
  <data key="d4">0.84</data>
  <data key="d5">Cube numbers appear as an example of number patterns; the general pattern concept precedes the specific cube-number pattern.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7">CH01_EN_PDF012_B007</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0003" target="MATH6_C0011">
  <data key="d4">0.83</data>
  <data key="d5">Powers of 2 are listed among various number sequences; students need the overarching idea of number patterns before learning this particular sequence.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7">CH01_EN_PDF005_B016</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0003" target="MATH6_C0014">
  <data key="d4">0.85</data>
  <data key="d5">The textbook explicitly links regular‑polygon side counts to a counting‑number sequence, indicating that grasping number patterns helps understand the polygon sequence.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7">CH01_EN_PDF011_B005;CH01_HI_PDF011_B003</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0003" target="MATH6_C0040">
  <data key="d4">0.82</data>
  <data key="d5">Palindrome numbers are presented as a specific type of number pattern; recognising general patterns helps learners identify palindromic numbers.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7">CH03_EN_PDF007_B004</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0003" target="MATH6_ADD001">
  <data key="d4">0.89</data>
  <data key="d5">The Virahanka numbers are introduced as a specific numerical pattern after the general discussion of number patterns; the evidence lists Virahanka numbers among other patterns, indicating the earlier unit provides the foundational idea of recognizing sequences.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7">CH01_EN_PDF013_B009;CH01_HI_PDF012_B008</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0004" target="MATH6_C0014">
  <data key="d4">0.85</data>
  <data key="d5">The regular‑polygon passage explicitly uses the idea of a shape sequence (a shape pattern) to relate polygon sides to counting numbers, indicating that grasping shape patterns precedes studying regular polygons.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
