</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0005" target="MATH6_C0008">
  <data key="d4">0.85</data>
  <data key="d5">Triangular numbers are introduced as a particular number sequence; understanding the general notion of sequences is a prerequisite for studying this specific sequence.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7">CH01_EN_PDF002_B014;CH01_EN_PDF005_B004</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0005" target="MATH6_C0009">
  <data key="d4">0.9</data>
  <data key="d5">Square numbers are presented as a specific example of a number sequence; students must first grasp the general idea of a sequence before identifying square numbers as that pattern.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7">CH01_EN_PDF002_B014;CH01_EN_PDF005_B005</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0005" target="MATH6_C0010">
  <data key="d4">0.9</data>
  <data key="d5">Cube numbers are listed among examples of number sequences, so understanding what a sequence is required prior to learning cube numbers.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7">CH01_EN_PDF002_B014;CH01_EN_PDF012_B007</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0005" target="MATH6_C0011">
  <data key="d4">0.9</data>
  <data key="d5">Powers of 2 are introduced as a particular number sequence; the general sequence concept must be known first.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7">CH01_EN_PDF002_B014;CH01_EN_PDF003_B003</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0005" target="MATH6_ADD001">
  <data key="d4">0.92</data>
  <data key="d5">Virahanka numbers are presented as a specific example of number sequences; students must first grasp the general idea of sequences before studying this particular family.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7">CH01_EN_PDF002_B014;CH01_EN_PDF003_B002;CH01_EN_PDF013_B009</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0005" target="MATH6_ADD002">
  <data key="d4">0.9</data>
  <data key="d5">The Collatz conjecture deals with generating a sequence from a rule; understanding what a sequence is precedes learning this conjecture.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7">CH01_EN_PDF002_B014;CH01_EN_PDF003_B002;CH03_EN_PDF014_B006;CH03_EN_PDF015_B001</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0006" target="MATH6_C0005">
  <data key="d4">0.8</data>
  <data key="d5">Odd numbers are presented as a specific example within the broader discussion of number sequences, implying that the concept of odd numbers is needed before they can be treated as a sequence.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7">CH01_EN_PDF003_B003</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0006" target="MATH6_C0009">
  <data key="d4">0.85</data>
  <data key="d5">The text shows that adding successive odd numbers produces perfect squares (1, 1+3=4, 1+3+5=9, …), indicating that grasping odd numbers is needed before understanding square numbers.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7">CH01_EN_PDF006_B005;CH01_HI_PDF006_B004</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0006" target="MATH6_C0044">
  <data key="d4">0.85</data>
  <data key="d5">The chapter on odd numbers (CH01) introduces the definition and examples of odd numbers. The later chapter (CH03) again deals with odd numbers within more complex contexts (e.g., odd‑number palindromes), implying that the earlier understanding is needed first.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7">CH01_EN_PDF003_B003;CH03_EN_PDF008_B005</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0006" target="MATH6_ADD002">
  <data key="d4">0.92</data>
  <data key="d5">The Collatz conjecture rule explicitly distinguishes between even and odd numbers; students must first recognise odd numbers to apply the "odd → 3n+1" step.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7">CH01_EN_PDF003_B003;CH03_EN_PDF015_B001</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0007" target="MATH6_C0005">
  <data key="d4">0.8</data>
  <data key="d5">Even numbers appear as a listed example of number sequences; recognizing the property of evenness is required before classifying them as a sequence.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7">CH01_EN_PDF003_B003</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0007" target="MATH6_C0044">
  <data key="d4">0.6</data>
  <data key="d5">Odd numbers are defined as numbers that are not even; therefore a clear understanding of even numbers helps learners grasp the notion of odd numbers.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7">CH01_EN_PDF003_B003;CH03_EN_PDF008_B005</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0007" target="MATH6_ADD002">
  <data key="d4">0.95</data>
  <data key="d5">The Collatz rule explicitly distinguishes between even and odd numbers, requiring students to recognise what an even number is before applying the ‘take half if even’ step.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7">CH01_EN_PDF003_B003;CH03_EN_PDF015_B001</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0009" target="MATH6_C0010">
  <data key="d4">0.75</data>
  <data key="d5">Square numbers (n²) are introduced earlier as a 2‑dimensional pattern and provide the basic idea of repeated multiplication that is extended to cubes (n³). This pedagogical ordering is typical in Grade 6 curricula.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7">CH01_EN_PDF005_B005;CH01_EN_PDF005_B008;CH01_EN_PDF012_B007;CH01_EN_PDF016_B016</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0009" target="MATH6_C0048">
  <data key="d4">0.55</data>
  <data key="d5">A magic square requires arranging numbers in a square grid and understanding that each row/column sums to the same total; familiarity with square numbers (grid concept) supports this. However, the provided material contains no explicit passage on magic squares, so the prerequisite link is inferred and needs verification.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7">CH01_EN_PDF005_B005</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0009" target="MATH6_C0050">
  <data key="d4">0.75</data>
  <data key="d5">Kaprekar numbers are defined using the square of a number and then splitting the result; therefore students must first understand square numbers before tackling Kaprekar numbers.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7">CH01_EN_PDF005_B005</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0012" target="MATH6_C0003">
  <data key="d4">0.7</data>
  <data key="d5">Understanding consecutive numbers (counting numbers that follow each other) is needed to recognise and extend number patterns, making it a prerequisite.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0012" target="MATH6_C0008">
  <data key="d4">0.88</data>
  <data key="d5">Triangular numbers are introduced as a derived sequence from counting (consecutive) numbers; students must first understand what consecutive numbers are to grasp the formation of triangular numbers.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7">CH01_EN_PDF003_B003;CH01_EN_PDF005_B004</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0012" target="MATH6_C0011">
  <data key="d4">0.78</data>
  <data key="d5">Consecutive numbers are a foundational notion of integer ordering; grasping them precedes learning the more specialized sequence of powers of 2.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0013" target="MATH6_C0003">
  <data key="d4">0.7</data>
  <data key="d5">Natural numbers form the basic set of counting numbers; recognising number patterns relies on this foundational concept.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0013" target="MATH6_C0006">
  <data key="d4">0.6</data>
  <data key="d5">Natural numbers (counting numbers) form the superset from which odd numbers are derived; pedagogically students learn natural numbers before classifying them as odd, though the textbook evidence is only a list, so the link is weak.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7">CH01_EN_PDF003_B003</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0013" target="MATH6_C0007">
  <data key="d4">0.68</data>
  <data key="d5">Natural numbers are the foundational counting set from which even numbers are defined; students must know natural numbers before classifying evens.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0013" target="MATH6_C0008">
  <data key="d4">0.86</data>
  <data key="d5">Natural numbers (counting numbers) form the basis for constructing triangular numbers; the textbook lists natural numbers before presenting triangular numbers, indicating a prerequisite relationship.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7">CH01_EN_PDF003_B003;CH01_EN_PDF005_B004</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0013" target="MATH6_C0009">
  <data key="d4">0.85</data>
  <data key="d5">Natural numbers are the basic counting numbers from which square numbers are defined (e.g., 1,4,9…). Understanding natural numbers is therefore pedagogically required before introducing square numbers.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7">CH01_EN_PDF005_B005;CH01_EN_PDF005_B008</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0013" target="MATH6_C0010">
  <data key="d4">0.88</data>
  <data key="d5">Cube numbers are defined as the third powers of natural numbers (1³, 2³, …). Students must first understand the set of natural (counting) numbers before they can form their cubes, making natural numbers a prerequisite.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0013" target="MATH6_C0011">
  <data key="d4">0.81</data>
  <data key="d5">Natural numbers form the basic counting set from which powers of 2 are derived; therefore natural numbers are a prerequisite for understanding powers of 2.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0013" target="MATH6_C0012">
  <data key="d4">0.8</data>
  <data key="d5">Students must first know what natural numbers are before they can identify or generate consecutive numbers, which are simply pairs of natural numbers differing by one.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0013" target="MATH6_C0014">
  <data key="d4">0.85</data>
  <data key="d5">Understanding natural (counting) numbers is needed to enumerate the sides of regular polygons; the textbook explicitly ties the sequence of side counts to the counting numbers starting at 3.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7">CH01_EN_PDF011_B005</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0013" target="MATH6_C0027">
  <data key="d4">0.86</data>
  <data key="d5">Understanding natural numbers (counting and basic arithmetic) is required to grasp that a reflex angle is measured in degrees and lies between 180° and 360°, which are numeric thresholds.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7">CH02_EN_PDF039_B004</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0013" target="MATH6_C0028">
  <data key="d4">0.84</data>
  <data key="d5">The concept of a complete angle (full turn = 360°) relies on knowing whole numbers and their values; natural numbers provide the basis for interpreting the 360‑degree measure.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7">CH02_EN_PDF048_B006</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0013" target="MATH6_C0029">
  <data key="d4">0.88</data>
  <data key="d5">Degree measurement uses unit counts (1°, 360°) that presuppose familiarity with natural numbers for counting and comparing quantities.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7">CH02_EN_PDF021_B001;CH02_EN_PDF021_B003</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0013" target="MATH6_C0030">
  <data key="d4">0.85</data>
  <data key="d5">A protractor is marked with degree numbers (0–180 or 0–360); interpreting these markings requires the prior understanding of natural numbers.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7">CH02_EN_PDF022_B005;CH02_EN_PDF023_B002</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0013" target="MATH6_C0036">
  <data key="d4">0.92</data>
  <data key="d5">Natural numbers are the foundational counting numbers; grasping them is required before introducing the broader concept of numbers and their operations in Chapter 3.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7">CH03_EN_PDF001_B001;CH03_EN_PDF001_B002</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0013" target="MATH6_C0037">
  <data key="d4">0.9</data>
  <data key="d5">Digits are symbols that represent natural numbers; students must first understand natural numbers to appreciate the role of individual digits in forming numbers.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7">CH03_EN_PDF003_B007;CH03_EN_PDF004_B015</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0013" target="MATH6_C0038">
  <data key="d4">0.93</data>
  <data key="d5">Place‑value concepts build on the idea of natural numbers and their representation; knowing natural numbers is a prerequisite for learning how each digit’s position determines its value.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0013" target="MATH6_C0039">
  <data key="d4">0.7</data>
  <data key="d5">Understanding natural numbers (counting, place value) is needed before students can compute the sum of a number's digits.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0013" target="MATH6_C0040">
  <data key="d4">0.7</data>
  <data key="d5">Recognising palindromic numbers requires familiarity with natural numbers and their digit representation, which is taught earlier.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0013" target="MATH6_C0041">
  <data key="d4">0.7</data>
  <data key="d5">Placing numbers on a number line assumes students know the magnitude and ordering of natural numbers.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0013" target="MATH6_C0042">
  <data key="d4">0.7</data>
  <data key="d5">Estimation tasks rely on an intuitive sense of size and magnitude of natural numbers, making natural numbers a prerequisite.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0013" target="MATH6_C0043">
  <data key="d4">0.9</data>
  <data key="d5">Natural numbers are the foundational counting set introduced in Chapter 1; rounding operations on numbers presuppose that learners can identify and compare natural numbers, making the former a prerequisite.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0013" target="MATH6_C0044">
  <data key="d4">0.9</data>
  <data key="d5">Understanding natural numbers is required before distinguishing odd numbers, as odd/even classification is defined for natural numbers. Hence natural numbers precede the concept of odd numbers.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0013" target="MATH6_C0045">
  <data key="d4">0.9</data>
  <data key="d5">Even numbers are a subset of natural numbers; students must first grasp natural numbers to recognize and work with even numbers, so natural numbers are a prerequisite.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0013" target="MATH6_C0046">
  <data key="d4">0.9</data>
  <data key="d5">Addition operates on natural numbers; learners need to understand the set of natural numbers before performing addition, making natural numbers a prerequisite for addition.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0013" target="MATH6_C0047">
  <data key="d4">0.82</data>
  <data key="d5">Subtraction operates on natural numbers; students must first understand what natural numbers are before performing subtraction.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7">CH03_EN_PDF001_B001</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0013" target="MATH6_C0050">
  <data key="d4">0.85</data>
  <data key="d5">Kaprekar numbers are a special class of natural numbers; students need the basic concept of natural numbers before exploring Kaprekar numbers.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0013" target="MATH6_C0051">
  <data key="d4">0.7</data>
  <data key="d5">Supercell tasks require working with 4‑digit natural numbers; therefore students must first understand natural numbers before attempting supercell exercises.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7">CH03_EN_PDF003_B005;CH03_EN_PDF003_B007</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0013" target="MATH6_ADD001">
  <data key="d4">0.75</data>
  <data key="d5">Virahanka numbers are defined as a sequence of natural numbers; grasping the concept of natural numbers is a prerequisite for learning this new number pattern.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7">CH01_EN_PDF013_B009;CH01_HI_PDF012_B008</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0013" target="MATH6_ADD002">
  <data key="d4">0.82</data>
  <data key="d5">Collatz conjecture works with sequences of natural numbers; students must first understand what natural numbers are before they can follow the rule of halving even numbers or applying 3n+1 to odd numbers.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7">CH03_EN_PDF015_B001;CH03_EN_PDF014_B006</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0015" target="MATH6_C0040">
  <data key="d4">0.7</data>
  <data key="d5">Recognizing visual patterns builds the general skill of spotting regularities, which is needed before students can identify the specific numeric pattern of palindromes.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7">CH03_EN_PDF007_B004;CH03_EN_PDF007_B005;CH03_HI_PDF007_B004;CH03_HI_PDF007_B005</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0015" target="MATH6_C0049">
  <data key="d4">0.75</data>
  <data key="d5">Visual pattern activities develop the ability to recognise regularities, which is pedagogically needed before introducing the more abstract notion of numerical patterns in Chapter 3.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7">CH03_EN_PDF001_B001;CH03_EN_PDF003_B011</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0015" target="MATH6_ADD001">
  <data key="d4">0.78</data>
  <data key="d5">Recognizing visual patterns is a foundational skill that prepares students to identify specific numeric sequences such as Virahanka numbers, which are presented as an example of a pattern in the text.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7">CH01_EN_PDF013_B009;CH01_HI_PDF012_B008</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0016" target="MATH6_C0014">
  <data key="d4">0.86</data>
  <data key="d5">Understanding a point (a precise location with no size) is a foundational geometric notion required to define vertices of regular polygons.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7">CH02_EN_PDF001_B002;CH01_EN_PDF010_B003</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0016" target="MATH6_C0017">
  <data key="d4">0.92</data>
  <data key="d5">A point is the most basic geometric entity; understanding points is required before introducing lines, which are defined using points as endpoints.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7">CH02_EN_PDF001_B002;CH02_EN_PDF001_B003;CH02_EN_PDF002_B003</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0016" target="MATH6_C0018">
  <data key="d4">0.95</data>
  <data key="d5">A line segment is defined as the shortest path joining two points; thus students must understand the concept of a point before learning line segments.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7">CH02_EN_PDF002_B003;CH02_HI_PDF042_B005</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0016" target="MATH6_C0019">
  <data key="d4">0.96</data>
  <data key="d5">A ray is described as a part of a line that starts at a point and extends indefinitely; grasping the notion of a point is required first.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7">CH02_EN_PDF003_B001;CH02_HI_PDF003_B001</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0016" target="MATH6_C0020">
  <data key="d4">0.94</data>
  <data key="d5">An angle is formed by two rays that share a common starting point (a point); therefore understanding points precedes learning angles.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7">CH02_EN_PDF005_B004;CH02_HI_PDF005_B003</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0016" target="MATH6_C0021">
  <data key="d4">0.95</data>
  <data key="d5">The vertex of an angle is defined as the point where the two rays meet; knowledge of points is a prerequisite for the vertex concept.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7">CH02_EN_PDF005_B004;CH02_HI_PDF005_B003</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0016" target="MATH6_C0022">
  <data key="d4">0.9</data>
  <data key="d5">A angle’s arms are defined as two rays sharing a common endpoint (vertex). Recognizing a point as a location with no size is required before identifying the vertex of an angle.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7">CH02_EN_PDF001_B003;CH02_HI_PDF005_B003</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0016" target="MATH6_C0023">
  <data key="d4">0.9</data>
  <data key="d5">A straight angle is described as a 180° turn formed by two rays meeting at a point. Understanding what a point (the vertex) is must precede learning the straight‑angle concept.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7">CH02_EN_PDF001_B003;CH02_HI_PDF015_B004</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0016" target="MATH6_C0024">
  <data key="d4">0.9</data>
  <data key="d5">A right angle is defined by two arms meeting at a common point. Grasping the notion of a point (the vertex) is therefore a prerequisite for the right‑angle concept.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7">CH02_EN_PDF001_B003;CH02_HI_PDF017_B001</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0016" target="MATH6_C0025">
  <data key="d4">0.9</data>
  <data key="d5">An acute angle is an angle less than a right angle, formed by two rays sharing a vertex. Knowing what a point (the vertex) is is needed before studying acute angles.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7">CH02_EN_PDF001_B003;CH02_HI_PDF019_B004</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0016" target="MATH6_C0026">
  <data key="d4">0.78</data>
  <data key="d5">A point provides the basic notion of location where two rays meet; understanding this is needed before classifying an angle as obtuse.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7">CH02_EN_PDF001_B002;CH02_EN_PDF019_B006</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0016" target="MATH6_C0027">
  <data key="d4">0.78</data>
  <data key="d5">A point is the common vertex of rays that form any angle, including reflex angles, so it must be understood first.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7">CH02_EN_PDF001_B002;CH02_EN_PDF039_B004</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0016" target="MATH6_C0028">
  <data key="d4">0.78</data>
  <data key="d5">To grasp a complete angle (full turn) learners need the notion of a point as the vertex of the angle.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7">CH02_EN_PDF001_B002;CH02_EN_PDF048_B006</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0016" target="MATH6_C0029">
  <data key="d4">0.78</data>
  <data key="d5">Degree is a unit for measuring angles; students must first understand what an angle is, which relies on the concept of a point as its vertex.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7">CH02_EN_PDF001_B002;CH02_EN_PDF021_B001</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0016" target="MATH6_C0030">
  <data key="d4">0.7</data>
  <data key="d5">Students must first grasp that a point marks a precise location (the vertex of an angle) before they can meaningfully place a protractor to measure that angle.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7">CH02_EN_PDF001_B002;CH02_EN_PDF022_B005</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0016" target="MATH6_C0031">
  <data key="d4">0.8</data>
  <data key="d5">Understanding a point is needed to identify where two lines meet; only then can the concept of perpendicular lines (right‑angle intersection) be introduced.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7">CH02_EN_PDF001_B002;CH02_EN_PDF017_B005</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0016" target="MATH6_C0034">
  <data key="d4">0.78</data>
  <data key="d5">A point is the basic geometric entity that serves as the vertex or centre for rotations; students must grasp the notion of a point before understanding rotation about a point.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7">CH02_EN_PDF001_B003;CH02_EN_PDF006_B005</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0016" target="MATH6_C0035">
  <data key="d4">0.81</data>
  <data key="d5">An angle bisector is defined with respect to an angle, which itself is formed by two rays meeting at a point (the vertex). Knowing what a point is precedes learning about bisecting angles.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7">CH02_EN_PDF001_B003;CH02_EN_PDF028_B003</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0016" target="MATH6_ADD003">
  <data key="d4">0.95</data>
  <data key="d5">Points are described as the basic building blocks of plane geometry, so students must grasp the notion of a point before studying plane geometry.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7">CH02_EN_PDF001_B002</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0017" target="MATH6_C0014">
  <data key="d4">0.84</data>
  <data key="d5">A line is the basic infinite extension through two points; regular polygons are composed of line segments, so grasping the concept of a line precedes constructing polygons.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7">CH02_EN_PDF001_B002;CH01_EN_PDF010_B003</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0017" target="MATH6_C0018">
  <data key="d4">0.85</data>
  <data key="d5">A line segment is defined as the shortest path between two points on a line; understanding the concept of a line (and points) precedes learning about line segments.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7">CH02_EN_PDF002_B003</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0017" target="MATH6_C0019">
  <data key="d4">0.9</data>
  <data key="d5">A ray is explicitly defined as a portion of a line that starts at a point and extends indefinitely, making knowledge of lines prerequisite.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7">CH02_EN_PDF003_B001</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0017" target="MATH6_C0021">
  <data key="d4">0.85</data>
  <data key="d5">The concept of a line (including points, rays, and line segments) is introduced first as a basic geometric building block. The vertex of an angle is defined later as the common starting point of two rays. Understanding lines/rays is therefore pedagogically required before learning what a vertex is.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7">CH02_EN_PDF001_B002;CH02_EN_PDF005_B004</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0017" target="MATH6_C0022">
  <data key="d4">0.84</data>
  <data key="d5">Lines (and rays) are presented as foundational geometry concepts. An arm of an angle is a ray that forms part of an angle, so students must first grasp lines/rays before identifying angle arms.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7">CH02_EN_PDF001_B002;CH02_HI_PDF005_B003</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0017" target="MATH6_C0023">
  <data key="d4">0.86</data>
  <data key="d5">A straight angle is defined as an angle formed by two opposite rays, essentially a continuation of a straight line. Hence, knowledge of lines (and rays) precedes the notion of a straight angle.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7">CH02_EN_PDF001_B002;CH02_EN_PDF015_B004</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0017" target="MATH6_C0024">
  <data key="d4">0.85</data>
  <data key="d5">Right angles are specific angles (90°) that are introduced after basic line and angle concepts. Understanding lines and the general idea of angles is required before distinguishing right angles.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7">CH02_EN_PDF001_B002;CH02_EN_PDF017_B001</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0017" target="MATH6_C0025">
  <data key="d4">0.9</data>
  <data key="d5">Lines (points, line segments) are introduced as basic building blocks of plane geometry, and angles are defined later using these elements, making line understanding prerequisite for acute angles.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7">CH02_EN_PDF001_B002</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0017" target="MATH6_C0026">
  <data key="d4">0.9</data>
  <data key="d5">The chapter presents lines and related concepts first, then classifies angles (acute, obtuse, etc.), so grasping lines is needed before learning about obtuse angles.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7">CH02_EN_PDF001_B002</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0017" target="MATH6_C0027">
  <data key="d4">0.9</data>
  <data key="d5">Understanding lines and line segments precedes the introduction of reflex angles; angles are built on the notion of rays/lines, so line knowledge is a prerequisite.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7">CH02_EN_PDF001_B002</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0017" target="MATH6_C0028">
  <data key="d4">0.9</data>
  <data key="d5">Lines are taught before the classification of angles, including the complete angle, making line concepts a necessary foundation for learning about complete angles.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7">CH02_EN_PDF001_B002</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0017" target="MATH6_C0029">
  <data key="d4">0.7</data>
  <data key="d5">Understanding what a line (and the angles formed by two lines) is required before introducing the unit of angle measurement (degree). The line entry introduces points, lines and angles, while the degree entry discusses measuring angles in degrees.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7">CH02_EN_PDF001_B002;CH02_EN_PDF021_B001</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0017" target="MATH6_C0030">
  <data key="d4">0.75</data>
  <data key="d5">A protractor is used to measure angles; recognizing angles presupposes knowledge of lines that form those angles. The line passage defines lines and angles, the protractor passage describes the tool for measuring them.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7">CH02_EN_PDF001_B002;CH02_EN_PDF022_B005</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0017" target="MATH6_C0031">
  <data key="d4">0.8</data>
  <data key="d5">Perpendicular lines are defined as two lines meeting at a right angle. Students must first understand what a line is before they can grasp the concept of perpendicularity.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7">CH02_EN_PDF001_B002;CH02_EN_PDF017_B005</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0017" target="MATH6_C0032">
  <data key="d4">0.8</data>
  <data key="d5">Parallel lines are a relationship between two lines that never meet. Knowledge of what a line is is a prerequisite for learning about parallelism.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7">CH02_EN_PDF001_B002</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0017" target="MATH6_C0033">
  <data key="d4">0.86</data>
  <data key="d5">Students must first recognise what a line is before they can understand the concept of two lines intersecting.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7">CH02_EN_PDF001_B002</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0017" target="MATH6_C0035">
  <data key="d4">0.84</data>
  <data key="d5">An angle bisector is defined as a line that divides an angle into two equal parts, so understanding what a line is precedes learning about its role as a bisector.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7">CH02_EN_PDF001_B002</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0017" target="MATH6_ADD003">
  <data key="d4">0.97</data>
  <data key="d5">The textbook states that points, lines, rays, line‑segments and angles are the building blocks of ‘plane geometry’, implying that understanding lines precedes understanding plane geometry.</data>
