</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0020" target="MATH6_C0022">
  <data key="d4">0.8</data>
  <data key="d5">An angle is described as being formed by two rays called the arms; understanding the general idea of an angle precedes learning the specific terminology for its arms.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7">CH02_EN_PDF005_B004</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0020" target="MATH6_C0023">
  <data key="d4">0.88</data>
  <data key="d5">A straight angle is a particular type of angle (180°). Mastery of the general angle concept is required before students can recognize and work with straight angles.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7">CH02_EN_PDF005_B004;CH02_EN_PDF015_B004</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0020" target="MATH6_C0024">
  <data key="d4">0.96</data>
  <data key="d5">Students must first grasp the general notion of an angle (its formation by two rays, vertex, arms) before they can recognise the special case of a right angle, which is defined as a specific measure of an angle.</data>
