</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0021" target="MATH6_C0026">
  <data key="d4">0.9</data>
  <data key="d5">Obtuse angles are defined relative to other angles; students must first understand the vertex concept that underlies any angle description.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7">CH02_EN_PDF005_B004;CH02_EN_PDF019_B006</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0021" target="MATH6_C0027">
  <data key="d4">0.9</data>
  <data key="d5">Knowing the vertex defines what an angle is; this is required before classifying angles such as reflex angles.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7">CH02_EN_PDF005_B004;CH02_EN_PDF039_B004</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0021" target="MATH6_C0028">
  <data key="d4">0.9</data>
  <data key="d5">Understanding the vertex (the basic definition of an angle) is needed before introducing the complete angle (360°).</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7">CH02_EN_PDF005_B004;CH02_EN_PDF048_B006</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0021" target="MATH6_C0029">
  <data key="d4">0.9</data>
  <data key="d5">The concept of a vertex is prerequisite to measuring angles in degrees, as degree is a unit of angle measurement.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7">CH02_EN_PDF005_B004;CH02_EN_PDF021_B001</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0021" target="MATH6_C0030">
  <data key="d4">0.9</data>
  <data key="d5">A student must first understand what an angle (its vertex) is before using a protractor to measure angles.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7">CH02_EN_PDF005_B004;CH02_EN_PDF022_B005</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0021" target="MATH6_C0031">
  <data key="d4">0.8</data>
  <data key="d5">Understanding that an angle has a vertex (the common point of two rays) is needed before recognizing that two lines meeting at a right angle share a common point, i.e., are perpendicular.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7">CH02_EN_PDF005_B004;CH02_EN_PDF017_B005</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0021" target="MATH6_C0033">
  <data key="d4">0.9</data>
  <data key="d5">Intersecting lines are defined as lines that meet at a point; recognizing that point as a vertex of an angle is a prerequisite.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7">CH02_EN_PDF005_B004</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0021" target="MATH6_C0034">
  <data key="d4">0.9</data>
  <data key="d5">The size of an angle is described as the amount of rotation about its vertex, so knowing what a vertex is precedes the concept of rotation.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7">CH02_EN_PDF005_B004;CH02_EN_PDF006_B005</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0021" target="MATH6_C0035">
  <data key="d4">0.9</data>
  <data key="d5">To define and work with an angle bisector, students must first recognise the angle’s vertex and arms; the vertex concept is introduced before the bisector activity.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7">CH02_EN_PDF005_B004;CH02_EN_PDF028_B003</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0021" target="MATH6_ADD003">
  <data key="d4">0.96</data>
  <data key="d5">The textbook states that points, lines, rays, and angles (which include the vertex) are the building blocks of ‘plane geometry’. Hence, grasping the concept of a vertex is pedagogically required before studying plane geometry as a whole.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7">CH02_EN_PDF005_B004;CH02_EN_PDF001_B002</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0022" target="MATH6_C0023">
  <data key="d4">0.9</data>
  <data key="d5">Students must first recognise that an angle consists of two arms (rays) meeting at a vertex before they can understand the special case of a straight angle, which is defined in terms of its arms forming a half turn.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7">CH02_HI_PDF005_B003;CH02_HI_PDF015_B004</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0022" target="MATH6_C0024">
  <data key="d4">0.9</data>
  <data key="d5">Identifying the arms of an angle is a prerequisite for classifying a right angle, which is described as one of the two equal angles formed when a straight angle is divided; this relies on the notion of arms.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7">CH02_HI_PDF005_B003;CH02_HI_PDF017_B001</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0022" target="MATH6_C0025">
  <data key="d4">0.9</data>
  <data key="d5">Understanding what an arm of an angle is precedes the ability to compare the size of angles, such as acute angles, which are defined relative to a right angle and thus depend on the concept of arms.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7">CH02_HI_PDF005_B003;CH02_HI_PDF019_B004</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0022" target="MATH6_C0026">
  <data key="d4">0.9</data>
  <data key="d5">The definition of an obtuse angle refers to the amount of turn between its arms; therefore, knowing what the arms are is required before learning about obtuse angles.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7">CH02_HI_PDF005_B003;CH02_HI_PDF019_B005</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0022" target="MATH6_C0027">
  <data key="d4">0.6</data>
  <data key="d5">Understanding the arms (sides) of an angle is needed to grasp what constitutes a reflex angle, which is defined in terms of the size of an angle.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7">CH02_HI_PDF005_B003;CH02_EN_PDF039_B004</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0022" target="MATH6_C0028">
  <data key="d4">0.6</data>
  <data key="d5">Knowing the arms of an angle is required before introducing the concept of a complete angle (360°), as a complete angle is a specific type of angle.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7">CH02_HI_PDF005_B003;CH02_EN_PDF048_B006</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0022" target="MATH6_C0029">
  <data key="d4">0.6</data>
  <data key="d5">The notion of angle arms underlies the definition of degree measure, which quantifies angles.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7">CH02_HI_PDF005_B003;CH02_EN_PDF021_B001</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0022" target="MATH6_C0030">
  <data key="d4">0.6</data>
  <data key="d5">Using a protractor to measure angles presupposes knowledge of what an angle’s arms are and where the vertex lies.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7">CH02_HI_PDF005_B003;CH02_EN_PDF022_B005</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0022" target="MATH6_C0031">
  <data key="d4">0.9</data>
  <data key="d5">Knowing what an arm (भुजा) of an angle is required to identify and describe a right angle, which is the basis for defining perpendicular lines.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7">CH02_HI_PDF005_B003;CH02_EN_PDF017_B005</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0022" target="MATH6_C0032">
  <data key="d4">0.85</data>
  <data key="d5">Understanding arms of an angle is needed before studying parallel lines, because properties of parallel lines are expressed using angles formed by intersecting lines and their arms.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7">CH02_HI_PDF005_B003</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0022" target="MATH6_C0033">
  <data key="d4">0.88</data>
  <data key="d5">Intersecting lines create angles; recognizing the arms of those angles is a prerequisite for learning about intersecting lines.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7">CH02_HI_PDF005_B003</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0022" target="MATH6_C0034">
  <data key="d4">0.92</data>
  <data key="d5">Rotation is defined as the amount of turn needed to move one arm of an angle to the other; thus the concept of an arm of an angle must be understood first.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7">CH02_HI_PDF005_B003;CH02_EN_PDF006_B005</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0022" target="MATH6_C0035">
  <data key="d4">0.88</data>
  <data key="d5">Students must first recognise the two sides (arms) and the vertex of an angle before they can understand how a line can divide that angle into two equal parts; the bisector definition builds on the notion of an angle’s arms.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7">CH02_HI_PDF005_B003;CH02_EN_PDF028_B003</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0022" target="MATH6_ADD003">
  <data key="d4">0.92</data>
  <data key="d5">Knowledge of angle arms is a basic component of plane geometry; the plane geometry chapter lists angles as foundational concepts, so mastering arms of an angle precedes broader plane geometry study.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7">CH02_HI_PDF005_B003;CH02_EN_PDF001_B002</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0023" target="MATH6_C0027">
  <data key="d4">0.8</data>
  <data key="d5">A reflex angle is explicitly defined as an angle greater than a straight angle and less than a full turn, making knowledge of straight angles a direct prerequisite.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7">CH02_EN_PDF039_B004</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0023" target="MATH6_C0028">
  <data key="d4">0.75</data>
  <data key="d5">Students must first recognise a straight angle (180°) before they can understand a complete angle (full turn, 360°) as twice a straight angle.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7">CH02_EN_PDF015_B004;CH02_EN_PDF048_B006</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0023" target="MATH6_C0029">
  <data key="d4">0.8</data>
  <data key="d5">The degree measure of a straight angle (180°) is explicitly given, so grasping the straight‑angle concept precedes learning the unit ‘degree’ and its application.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7">CH02_EN_PDF015_B004;CH02_EN_PDF021_B001</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0023" target="MATH6_C0030">
  <data key="d4">0.75</data>
  <data key="d5">A protractor is introduced as a tool that divides a straight angle (180°) into 1° units; thus understanding the straight angle is needed first.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7">CH02_EN_PDF015_B004;CH02_EN_PDF022_B005</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0023" target="MATH6_C0031">
  <data key="d4">0.8</data>
  <data key="d5">Perpendicular lines form a right angle, defined as half of a straight angle; therefore the straight‑angle concept underpins the notion of perpendicularity.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7">CH02_EN_PDF015_B004;CH02_EN_PDF017_B005</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0023" target="MATH6_ADD003">
  <data key="d4">0.93</data>
  <data key="d5">Plane geometry is introduced as built on basic ideas of geometry, including angles. Understanding a straight angle is part of that foundational angle knowledge, making it a prerequisite for grasping plane geometry concepts.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7">CH02_EN_PDF015_B004;CH02_EN_PDF001_B002</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0024" target="MATH6_C0025">
  <data key="d4">0.94</data>
  <data key="d5">Acute angles are defined as angles less than a right angle. Therefore, students must first understand the concept of a right angle to recognize and classify acute angles.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7">CH02_EN_PDF017_B001;CH02_EN_PDF019_B006</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0024" target="MATH6_C0026">
  <data key="d4">0.95</data>
  <data key="d5">Obtuse angles are defined as greater than a right angle but less than a straight angle, so students must understand right angles before classifying obtuse angles.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7">CH02_EN_PDF019_B006</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0024" target="MATH6_C0028">
  <data key="d4">0.9</data>
  <data key="d5">A complete angle (full turn) is described as consisting of four right angles, so understanding right angles is needed before grasping the concept of a complete angle.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7">CH02_EN_PDF048_B006</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0024" target="MATH6_C0030">
  <data key="d4">0.7</data>
  <data key="d5">Students must first recognise a right angle (90°) before they can meaningfully use a protractor to measure angles in degrees.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7">CH02_EN_PDF017_B001;CH02_EN_PDF017_B004;CH02_EN_PDF022_B005;CH02_EN_PDF023_B002</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0024" target="MATH6_C0031">
  <data key="d4">0.95</data>
  <data key="d5">The definition of perpendicular lines explicitly relies on the concept of a right angle (“Two lines that meet at right angles are called perpendicular lines”).</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7">CH02_EN_PDF017_B001;CH02_EN_PDF017_B004;CH02_EN_PDF017_B005</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0028" target="MATH6_C0027">
  <data key="d4">0.94</data>
  <data key="d5">A reflex angle is defined as an angle greater than a straight angle but less than a complete (whole) angle; thus understanding the complete angle is prerequisite.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7">CH02_EN_PDF039_B004;CH02_EN_PDF048_B006</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0028" target="MATH6_C0032">
  <data key="d4">0.7</data>
  <data key="d5">Understanding a complete angle (360°) provides the basis for measuring and classifying angles, which is needed before studying angles formed by parallel lines and transversals.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7">CH02_EN_PDF048_B006</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0028" target="MATH6_C0033">
  <data key="d4">0.7</data>
  <data key="d5">Knowing what a complete angle is helps learners grasp the concept of angles created where two lines intersect, making it a prerequisite for intersecting‑lines topics.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7">CH02_EN_PDF048_B006</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0029" target="MATH6_C0026">
  <data key="d4">0.78</data>
  <data key="d5">Understanding the unit of measurement (degree) is needed to quantify any angle, including obtuse angles; thus degree knowledge precedes classifying obtuse angles.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7">CH02_EN_PDF021_B001;CH02_EN_PDF021_B003;CH02_EN_PDF019_B006</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0029" target="MATH6_C0027">
  <data key="d4">0.93</data>
  <data key="d5">The definition of a reflex angle uses degree measures (greater than 180° and less than 360°), so knowledge of degrees is needed first.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7">CH02_EN_PDF039_B004;CH02_EN_PDF021_B001</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0029" target="MATH6_C0028">
  <data key="d4">0.85</data>
  <data key="d5">The concept of degree (360° for a full turn) is required to define and understand a complete angle.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7">CH02_EN_PDF021_B001;CH02_EN_PDF048_B006</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0029" target="MATH6_C0030">
  <data key="d4">0.9</data>
  <data key="d5">The concept of degree as a unit of angular measure is required to understand how a protractor marks and measures angles.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7">CH02_EN_PDF021_B001;CH02_EN_PDF022_B005</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0029" target="MATH6_C0031">
  <data key="d4">0.93</data>
  <data key="d5">Students must first learn that a degree measures angle size and that a right angle equals 90°, which is essential to recognise perpendicular lines as those meeting at a right angle.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7">CH02_EN_PDF021_B001;CH02_EN_PDF021_B003;CH02_EN_PDF017_B005</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0029" target="MATH6_C0034">
  <data key="d4">0.88</data>
  <data key="d5">Understanding that an angle’s measure in degrees corresponds to the amount of rotation is required before students can grasp the concept of rotation itself.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7">CH02_EN_PDF021_B001;CH02_EN_PDF021_B003;CH02_EN_PDF006_B004;CH02_EN_PDF006_B005</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0029" target="MATH6_C0035">
  <data key="d4">0.75</data>
  <data key="d5">Students must first understand that angles are measured in degrees before they can meaningfully construct or identify a line that divides an angle into two equal parts (angle bisector).</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7">CH02_EN_PDF021_B001;CH02_EN_PDF021_B003;CH02_EN_PDF028_B003</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0030" target="MATH6_C0027">
  <data key="d4">0.7</data>
  <data key="d5">Students first learn to use a protractor to measure angles; only then can they classify angles such as reflex (&gt;180°). The protractor passage explains measurement, while the reflex‑angle passage defines the range, indicating the tool is a prerequisite.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7">CH02_EN_PDF022_B005;CH02_EN_PDF039_B004</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0030" target="MATH6_C0031">
  <data key="d4">0.86</data>
  <data key="d5">Students must first learn how to measure angles in degrees with a protractor before they can identify a right angle (half of a straight angle) and understand perpendicular lines.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7">CH02_EN_PDF022_B005;CH02_EN_PDF017_B005</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0030" target="MATH6_C0035">
  <data key="d4">0.88</data>
  <data key="d5">Students must first understand what a protractor is and how to read degrees before they can meaningfully construct or identify an angle bisector, which relies on halving an angle measured with a protractor.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7">CH02_EN_PDF022_B005;CH02_EN_PDF028_B003</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0033" target="MATH6_C0031">
  <data key="d4">0.8</data>
  <data key="d5">Intersecting lines are the basic notion that two lines meet; perpendicular lines are a specific case where the intersecting angle is a right angle, so knowing intersecting lines precedes the perpendicular definition.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7">CH02_EN_PDF017_B005</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0033" target="MATH6_C0032">
  <data key="d4">0.6</data>
  <data key="d5">To grasp the definition of parallel lines, students must first know what it means for two lines to intersect (i.e., meet at a point). However, the supplied material provides no explicit passages linking the two concepts, so the judgment is tentative.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0033" target="MATH6_C0034">
  <data key="d4">0.86</data>
  <data key="d5">Understanding intersecting lines provides the notion of an angle formed at the intersection, which is essential before defining an angle as a measure of rotation.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7">CH02_EN_PDF006_B004;CH02_EN_PDF006_B005;CH02_HI_PDF006_B003;CH02_HI_PDF006_B007</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0033" target="MATH6_C0035">
  <data key="d4">0.88</data>
  <data key="d5">Angle bisectors are defined with respect to an angle created by two intersecting lines; thus knowledge of intersecting lines is a prerequisite for learning about angle bisectors.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7">CH02_EN_PDF028_B003;CH02_EN_PDF028_B004;CH02_HI_PDF028_B003</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0034" target="MATH6_C0014">
  <data key="d4">0.7</data>
  <data key="d5">To grasp the interior angles of regular polygons, students must first understand that an angle is a measure of rotation; thus the concept of rotation precedes regular polygons.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7">CH02_EN_PDF006_B005;CH01_EN_PDF011_B005</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0034" target="MATH6_C0023">
  <data key="d4">0.78</data>
  <data key="d5">The rotation passages define an angle as the amount of turn required, a more fundamental notion that precedes classifying specific angles such as a straight angle (180° rotation). Thus understanding rotation is pedagogically needed before identifying a straight angle.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7">CH02_EN_PDF006_B004;CH02_EN_PDF006_B005;CH02_HI_PDF006_B003;CH02_HI_PDF006_B007</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0034" target="MATH6_C0024">
  <data key="d4">0.86</data>
  <data key="d5">Students must first grasp that an angle’s size is measured by the amount of rotation (turn) before they can identify a specific angle such as a right angle (½ of a full turn). The rotation passage defines angle size as rotation, which is used in the right‑angle passage to relate it to a half turn.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7">CH02_EN_PDF006_B004;CH02_EN_PDF006_B005;CH02_EN_PDF017_B004</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0034" target="MATH6_C0025">
  <data key="d4">0.85</data>
  <data key="d5">The concept of rotation (amount of turn) is explicitly defined as the basis for measuring angles. Classifying an angle as acute relies on comparing its rotation to that of a right angle, so understanding rotation precedes the notion of acute angles.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7">CH02_EN_PDF006_B004;CH02_EN_PDF006_B005;CH02_HI_PDF006_B003;CH02_HI_PDF006_B007</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0034" target="MATH6_C0026">
  <data key="d4">0.9</data>
  <data key="d5">The concept of rotation defines the size of an angle as the amount of turn, which is foundational for classifying angles such as obtuse. Thus rotation must be understood before learning about obtuse angles.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7">CH02_EN_PDF006_B004;CH02_EN_PDF006_B005</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0034" target="MATH6_C0027">
  <data key="d4">0.88</data>
  <data key="d5">Understanding rotation (the amount of turn that defines an angle) is needed before classifying angles such as reflex angles, which are defined by their measure (&gt;180°).</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7">CH02_EN_PDF006_B004;CH02_EN_PDF006_B005;CH02_EN_PDF039_B004</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0034" target="MATH6_C0028">
  <data key="d4">0.6</data>
  <data key="d5">The notion of rotation (the amount of turn) is used to define the size of an angle; therefore students need the rotation idea before they can understand a complete (full‑turn) angle.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7">CH02_EN_PDF006_B004;CH02_EN_PDF006_B005</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0034" target="MATH6_C0030">
  <data key="d4">0.84</data>
  <data key="d5">The notion of an angle as an amount of rotation is presented before the tool (protractor) used to measure that rotation; thus understanding rotation is prerequisite for using a protractor.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7">CH02_EN_PDF006_B004;CH02_EN_PDF006_B005;CH02_EN_PDF022_B005</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0034" target="MATH6_C0031">
  <data key="d4">0.85</data>
  <data key="d5">Understanding angle size as rotation is needed before defining a right angle (half a straight angle), which is the basis for perpendicular lines.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7">CH02_EN_PDF006_B004;CH02_EN_PDF006_B005</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0034" target="MATH6_C0035">
  <data key="d4">0.88</data>
  <data key="d5">Understanding rotation defines what an angle is; this is needed before learning how to bisect an angle. The rotation passage explains angle size as amount of rotation, and the bisector passage builds on that concept.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7">CH02_EN_PDF006_B004;CH02_EN_PDF006_B005;CH02_EN_PDF028_B003;CH02_EN_PDF028_B004</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0034" target="MATH6_ADD003">
  <data key="d4">0.81</data>
  <data key="d5">Plane geometry builds on basic angle concepts; the definition of an angle as a rotation is required before students can work with angles in plane‑geometry contexts.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7">CH02_EN_PDF006_B004;CH02_EN_PDF006_B005;CH02_EN_PDF001_B002</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0036" target="MATH6_C0003">
  <data key="d4">0.88</data>
  <data key="d5">Students must first understand what numbers are and how they are used before they can analyse patterns formed by those numbers.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7">CH03_EN_PDF001_B001</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0036" target="MATH6_C0005">
  <data key="d4">0.85</data>
  <data key="d5">The chapter on numbers introduces counting and basic operations, which are foundational for recognizing and working with number sequences.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7">CH01_EN_PDF002_B014</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0036" target="MATH6_C0006">
  <data key="d4">0.96</data>
  <data key="d5">A general understanding of numbers (counting, operations) is required before students can study special categories such as odd numbers.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7">CH03_EN_PDF001_B001</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0036" target="MATH6_C0008">
  <data key="d4">0.91</data>
  <data key="d5">The chapter on numbers introduces counting, addition, and basic operations that are essential for recognizing and generating triangular numbers, making the number concept a prerequisite.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7">CH03_EN_PDF001_B001;CH01_EN_PDF003_B003</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0036" target="MATH6_C0009">
  <data key="d4">0.85</data>
  <data key="d5">A basic understanding of numbers is required before learning the specific concept of square numbers; the general number chapter precedes the square‑number topic.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7">CH03_EN_PDF001_B001;CH01_EN_PDF005_B005</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0036" target="MATH6_C0010">
  <data key="d4">0.78</data>
  <data key="d5">Cube numbers are a specific class of numbers; students must first understand the general notion of numbers before recognizing cube numbers as a subset.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7">CH01_EN_PDF012_B007;CH03_EN_PDF001_B001</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0036" target="MATH6_C0011">
  <data key="d4">0.8</data>
  <data key="d5">Chapter 3 introduces basic number concepts, counting and operations that are required to compute and understand powers of 2. Hence understanding numbers precedes learning powers of 2.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7">CH03_EN_PDF001_B001</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0036" target="MATH6_C0012">
  <data key="d4">0.82</data>
  <data key="d5">Students must first understand what a number is before they can grasp the idea of two numbers that follow each other consecutively.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7">CH03_EN_PDF001_B001</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0036" target="MATH6_C0014">
  <data key="d4">0.9</data>
  <data key="d5">Counting the sides of regular polygons relies on the basic concept of numbers; students must be comfortable with numbers before they can relate them to polygon side counts.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7">CH03_EN_PDF001_B001</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0036" target="MATH6_C0028">
  <data key="d4">0.8</data>
  <data key="d5">Number concepts (counting, operations) are taught before geometric concepts like complete angle; students need basic number sense to understand fractions such as ¼ of a complete angle.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0036" target="MATH6_C0039">
  <data key="d4">0.84</data>
  <data key="d5">Calculating the digit sum requires first having a number (and its digits); thus understanding numbers is a prerequisite for the digit‑sum activity.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7">CH03_EN_PDF006_B009</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0036" target="MATH6_C0040">
  <data key="d4">0.9</data>
  <data key="d5">Students must first understand what numbers are before identifying special types such as palindromic numbers.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7">CH03_EN_PDF001_B001;CH03_EN_PDF007_B004</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0036" target="MATH6_C0041">
  <data key="d4">0.9</data>
  <data key="d5">Using a number line requires the prior concept of numbers and their values.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7">CH03_EN_PDF001_B001;CH03_EN_PDF005_B004</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0036" target="MATH6_C0042">
  <data key="d4">0.85</data>
  <data key="d5">Estimation tasks rely on the ability to recognise and manipulate numbers, so numbers are a prerequisite.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7">CH03_EN_PDF001_B001;CH03_EN_PDF016_B002</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0036" target="MATH6_C0043">
  <data key="d4">0.6</data>
  <data key="d5">Rounding involves approximating numbers, which presupposes basic number knowledge, but the supplied passages do not directly discuss rounding.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0036" target="MATH6_C0044">
  <data key="d4">0.95</data>
  <data key="d5">Understanding the general notion of numbers is required before distinguishing odd numbers, which are a specific subset of numbers.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7">CH03_EN_PDF001_B001</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0036" target="MATH6_C0045">
  <data key="d4">0.95</data>
  <data key="d5">Students must first grasp what numbers are before they can identify even numbers, a particular class of numbers.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7">CH03_EN_PDF001_B001</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0036" target="MATH6_C0046">
  <data key="d4">0.95</data>
  <data key="d5">The concept of numbers underlies the operation of addition; learners need to know what numbers are before performing addition.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7">CH03_EN_PDF001_B001</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0036" target="MATH6_C0047">
  <data key="d4">0.95</data>
  <data key="d5">Understanding numbers is a prerequisite for subtraction, as subtraction operates on numbers.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7">CH03_EN_PDF001_B001</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0036" target="MATH6_C0048">
  <data key="d4">0.92</data>
  <data key="d5">Students must first understand what numbers are and how to use basic operations before they can arrange numbers in a magic square, which relies on arithmetic properties of numbers.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7">CH03_EN_PDF001_B001;CH03_HI_PDF001_B001</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0036" target="MATH6_C0049">
  <data key="d4">0.88</data>
  <data key="d5">Recognising and extending numeric patterns presupposes familiarity with numbers and their basic operations, making the number concept a prerequisite for pattern work.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7">CH03_EN_PDF001_B001;CH03_HI_PDF001_B001</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0036" target="MATH6_C0050">
  <data key="d4">0.94</data>
  <data key="d5">Kaprekar numbers are a special class of numbers; learners need a solid grasp of ordinary numbers before they can explore this advanced property.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7">CH03_EN_PDF001_B001;CH03_HI_PDF001_B001</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0036" target="MATH6_C0051">
  <data key="d4">0.9</data>
  <data key="d5">The concept of a supercell is defined within a table of numbers; understanding numbers and basic arithmetic is required to identify and work with supercells.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7">CH03_EN_PDF001_B001;CH03_HI_PDF001_B001</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0036" target="MATH6_ADD001">
  <data key="d4">0.9</data>
  <data key="d5">Students must first understand what numbers are and how to use basic operations before encountering a specific numeric sequence such as Virahanka numbers.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7">CH03_EN_PDF001_B001;CH01_EN_PDF013_B009</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0036" target="MATH6_ADD002">
  <data key="d4">0.85</data>
  <data key="d5">The Collatz conjecture manipulates arbitrary numbers (halving even numbers, applying 3n+1 to odd numbers); grasping the notion of numbers is required first.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7">CH03_EN_PDF001_B001;CH03_EN_PDF014_B006</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0037" target="MATH6_C0003">
  <data key="d4">0.86</data>
  <data key="d5">The concept of a digit (individual symbols 0‑9) underlies any discussion of number patterns; therefore digit knowledge is required first.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7">CH03_EN_PDF003_B007</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0037" target="MATH6_C0005">
  <data key="d4">0.85</data>
  <data key="d5">Digits and place‑value concepts are required to read and interpret the numbers that appear in sequences.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7">CH01_EN_PDF002_B014</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0037" target="MATH6_C0009">
  <data key="d4">0.7</data>
  <data key="d5">Digits are a more basic notion required to read and write numbers, which students must master before they can interpret square numbers presented as 1,4,9,…</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7">CH03_EN_PDF003_B007;CH03_EN_PDF004_B015;CH01_EN_PDF005_B005</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0037" target="MATH6_C0012">
  <data key="d4">0.78</data>
  <data key="d5">Recognising individual digits (0‑9) is a prerequisite for forming numbers and then for seeing how one number follows another as consecutive numbers.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7">CH03_EN_PDF003_B007</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0037" target="MATH6_C0028">
  <data key="d4">0.8</data>
  <data key="d5">Understanding digits is a foundational number skill required before introducing angles, which involve fractional parts of a circle.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0037" target="MATH6_C0036">
  <data key="d4">0.81</data>
  <data key="d5">Digits (0‑9) are the symbols that compose numbers; students must recognise digits before they can understand whole numbers.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7">CH03_EN_PDF003_B007;CH03_EN_PDF004_B015</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0037" target="MATH6_C0038">
  <data key="d4">0.8</data>
  <data key="d5">Understanding individual digits is necessary before students can grasp place‑value, where the value of a digit depends on its position in a number.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7">CH03_EN_PDF003_B007</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0037" target="MATH6_C0039">
  <data key="d4">0.9</data>
  <data key="d5">Students must first understand what a digit is before they can add the digits of a number to find its digit sum.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7">CH03_EN_PDF003_B007;CH03_EN_PDF006_B009</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0037" target="MATH6_C0040">
  <data key="d4">0.9</data>
  <data key="d5">Recognising a palindrome requires reading a number digit‑by‑digit from left to right and right to left, which presupposes knowledge of digits.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7">CH03_EN_PDF003_B007;CH03_EN_PDF007_B004</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0037" target="MATH6_C0041">
  <data key="d4">0.9</data>
  <data key="d5">Placing numbers on a number line relies on understanding the magnitude of numbers, which is built on the concept of digits and place value.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7">CH03_EN_PDF003_B007;CH03_EN_PDF005_B004</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0037" target="MATH6_C0042">
  <data key="d4">0.9</data>
  <data key="d5">Estimating quantities of numbers uses knowledge of digit positions and values; thus digit knowledge precedes estimation activities.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7">CH03_EN_PDF003_B007;CH03_EN_PDF016_B002</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0037" target="MATH6_C0043">
  <data key="d4">0.7</data>
  <data key="d5">Understanding digits (place value) is needed before students can grasp rounding, which relies on identifying the value of each digit to decide which place to round to.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7">CH03_EN_PDF003_B007;CH03_EN_PDF004_B015</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0037" target="MATH6_C0044">
  <data key="d4">0.7</data>
  <data key="d5">Classifying a number as odd or even depends on the unit digit; therefore knowledge of digits precedes the concept of odd numbers.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7">CH03_EN_PDF003_B007;CH03_EN_PDF004_B015</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0037" target="MATH6_C0045">
  <data key="d4">0.7</data>
  <data key="d5">Identifying even numbers requires recognizing the units digit; thus the digit concept is a prerequisite for the even‑number concept.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7">CH03_EN_PDF003_B007;CH03_EN_PDF004_B015</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0037" target="MATH6_C0046">
  <data key="d4">0.7</data>
  <data key="d5">Performing multi‑digit addition correctly uses place‑value knowledge, which is built on the digit concept; hence digits are prerequisite to addition.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7">CH03_EN_PDF003_B007;CH03_EN_PDF004_B015</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0037" target="MATH6_C0047">
  <data key="d4">0.85</data>
  <data key="d5">Understanding digits and place value is needed to perform multi‑digit subtraction correctly, making digit knowledge a prerequisite for subtraction.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7">CH03_EN_PDF003_B007;CH03_EN_PDF004_B015;CH03_EN_PDF001_B001;CH03_EN_PDF012_B004</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0037" target="MATH6_C0050">
  <data key="d4">0.65</data>
  <data key="d5">Kaprekar numbers involve splitting a number into its constituent digits after squaring; thus knowledge of digits is a prerequisite.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7">CH03_EN_PDF003_B007;CH03_EN_PDF004_B015</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0037" target="MATH6_C0051">
  <data key="d4">0.85</data>
  <data key="d5">Students must first understand what a digit is and how to form multi‑digit numbers before they can compare numbers in a grid to identify a supercell (a cell whose number is larger than its neighbours). The activities that introduce supercells explicitly require filling cells with 4‑digit numbers, showing the prerequisite relationship.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7">CH03_EN_PDF003_B007;CH03_EN_PDF003_B005</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0038" target="MATH6_C0003">
  <data key="d4">0.84</data>
  <data key="d5">Place‑value explains the positional meaning of digits, which is essential before students can recognise systematic number patterns.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0038" target="MATH6_C0005">
  <data key="d4">0.85</data>
  <data key="d5">Place‑value understanding is a prerequisite for working with the numerical terms of a sequence.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7">CH01_EN_PDF002_B014</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0038" target="MATH6_C0011">
  <data key="d4">0.7</data>
  <data key="d5">Place value is a foundational concept for understanding the magnitude and representation of numbers, which underlies the notion of powers of 2; thus it is typically taught before exponentiation.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0038" target="MATH6_C0028">
  <data key="d4">0.8</data>
  <data key="d5">Place value is a core number concept that precedes geometry; grasping place value helps students work with fractions of a complete angle.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0038" target="MATH6_C0039">
  <data key="d4">0.7</data>
  <data key="d5">Students must first understand the value of each digit (place value) before they can meaningfully add the digits of a number, which is the digit‑sum operation.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0038" target="MATH6_C0041">
  <data key="d4">0.75</data>
  <data key="d5">Placing numbers accurately on a number line demands an understanding of their magnitude, which is built from place‑value knowledge of the digits.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0038" target="MATH6_C0043">
  <data key="d4">0.85</data>
  <data key="d5">Rounding numbers to the nearest ten, hundred, etc., requires students to recognise the value of each digit, i.e., place value, before they can apply rounding rules.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0038" target="MATH6_C0046">
  <data key="d4">0.8</data>
  <data key="d5">Performing addition with multi‑digit numbers (carrying/borrowing) presupposes that learners understand the positional value of each digit, making place value a prerequisite for addition.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7">CH03_EN_PDF001_B001;CH03_EN_PDF007_B009;CH03_HI_PDF001_B001;CH03_HI_PDF007_B007</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0038" target="MATH6_C0047">
  <data key="d4">0.8</data>
  <data key="d5">Multi‑digit subtraction requires students to understand the positional (place) values of digits to borrow and align numbers correctly, making place value a pedagogical prerequisite for subtraction.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7">CH03_EN_PDF001_B001</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0038" target="MATH6_C0050">
  <data key="d4">0.7</data>
  <data key="d5">Identifying a Kaprekar number involves splitting a squared number into parts based on digit positions, which presupposes an understanding of place value.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0038" target="MATH6_C0051">
  <data key="d4">0.85</data>
  <data key="d5">Supercell tasks require students to work with 4‑digit numbers and identify specific digit positions, which presupposes understanding of place value.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7">CH03_EN_PDF003_B005;CH03_EN_PDF003_B007</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0039" target="MATH6_C0028">
  <data key="d4">0.8</data>
  <data key="d5">Digit‑sum exercises rely on number manipulation skills that are taught before geometry; thus number concepts are prerequisite for understanding complete angles.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0041" target="MATH6_C0003">
  <data key="d4">0.85</data>
  <data key="d5">Understanding the number line (placement of numbers on a scale) is a foundational numeric skill that precedes exploring abstract number patterns.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7">CH03_EN_PDF005_B004</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0041" target="MATH6_C0011">
  <data key="d4">0.65</data>
  <data key="d5">Understanding the number line (ordering and magnitude of numbers) is generally established before introducing exponential growth such as powers of 2, making it a prerequisite.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0043" target="MATH6_C0042">
  <data key="d4">0.62</data>
  <data key="d5">Rounding to the nearest ten, hundred, etc., is a concrete technique used when students make estimations; therefore mastering rounding precedes broader estimation activities.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7">CH03_EN_PDF016_B002;CH03_EN_PDF017_B004</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0044" target="MATH6_C0051">
  <data key="d4">0.92</data>
  <data key="d5">The supercell activity explicitly uses the notion of odd numbers of cells (1,3,5,7,…) to determine supercell counts, indicating that grasping odd numbers is needed first.</data>
  <data key="d6">PENDING</data>
  <data key="d7">CH03_EN_PDF022_B012</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MATH6_C0044" target="MATH6_ADD002">
  <data key="d4">0.85</data>
  <data key="d5">The Collatz conjecture explicitly distinguishes between even and odd numbers in its rule, so grasping what an odd number is is a direct prerequisite for applying the conjecture.</data>
